#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184904} :activity/id #uuid "294c5c60-7cc0-5d4b-b33a-c4b1d0febcf6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048358 :topic/id #uuid "985dd262-d15d-59ca-814a-d63fefbc5327" :topic/math-academy-id 3829} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184140 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184050 :question/id #uuid "62e7eb47-4217-5bf0-b458-6059c1040fd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3476"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50af360c-635c-5b48-bfdf-8e85cc3608b2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184490}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Tangent of a Large Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"} :step/id #uuid "5e63ab9a-476f-5df8-9300-eb8b953d3e5d" :step/math-academy-id 11655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185312 :tutorial/content "Recall that the **reference angle** $\\theta_R$ of a given angle $\\theta$ is the positive acute angle $\\theta$ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nReference angles can be used to evaluate trigonometric functions for large angles. In this lesson, we'll learn how to express trigonometric ratios of large angles in terms of reference angles.\n\nAs an example, let's write the following trigonometric ratio in terms of a reference angle:\n\n$$\n\\sin{440^\\circ}\n$$\n\nTo express this trigonometric ratio in terms of a reference angle, we first find the corresponding *coterminal angle* that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$. We do this by subtracting integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ as many times as necessary until we get our coterminal angle:\n\n$$\n440^\\circ - 360^\\circ = 80^\\circ \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that our coterminal angle is acute and lies in the first quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 80^\\circ\n$$\n\nand we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin 440^\\circ = \\sin 80^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9d1ea107c7247c576a3b8b431b822ca08c8aee55c5898d59e48c6c4581188452.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1be22cf7-45e4-5f98-b822-b15908dfde21" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2719 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2a9925ca-b285-52f9-be38-c12b5a590da1" :step/math-academy-id 13279 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185002}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184216 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184121 :question/id #uuid "15f7c53c-eb3f-516a-9452-330dddee8a15" :question/math-academy-id "e-9956"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d16d09c-098e-5104-8703-9b2dcaf9e253" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051050}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184822}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Large Angles in the First Quadrant in Terms of Reference Angles"} :step/id #uuid "fb0249ac-20ee-5461-bef6-c7ec304fddc7" :step/math-academy-id 37490 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185341 :tutorial/content "Suppose we're given a large angle $\\theta$ that does *not* lie in the first quadrant. In these cases, the procedure for expressing a trigonometric ratio evaluated at $\\theta$ in terms of $\\theta_R$ is similar to what we saw previously. However, we also need to consider the *sign* of the trigonometric ratio in the quadrant where $\\theta$ lies.\n\nAs an example, let's express the following trigonometric ratio in terms of a reference angle:\n\n$$\n\\sin{940^\\circ}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we follow three steps:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find an angle that's coterminal with $940^\\circ$ and lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$. We do this by subtracting integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get our desired angle:\n$940^{∘} - 360^{∘}|= 580^{∘}; 940^{∘} - 2 \\cdot 360^{∘}|= 220^{∘}✓$\nTherefore,\n$\\sin 940^\\circ = \\sin 220^\\circ$.\nA sketch of our angle and its corresponding reference angle is shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b256260541503fdf451f29ef0c71cd4e8ef63883f4e1dbf93da071eda4aecd3.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute the reference angle. Since $\\theta = 220^\\circ$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle is\n$\\theta_R = 220^\\circ - 180^\\circ = 40^\\circ$.\n\n- **Step 3**: Determine the correct sign. The sine function is negative $({\\color{red}{-}})$ in the third quadrant. Therefore,\n$\\sin (220^\\circ) = {\\color{red}{-}}\\sin\\theta_R = {\\color{red}{-}}\\sin{40^\\circ}$.\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin 940^\\circ = -\\sin{40^\\circ}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b306b1d4-2c03-5cb7-9f03-07c8b3a7a420" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6184 :tutorial/title "Large Angles Outside the First Quadrant"} :step/id #uuid "a7946eab-4eb9-5b3a-833c-ef1c159c81ca" :step/math-academy-id 37492 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184217 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184122 :question/id #uuid "b70919c3-c48f-55c8-bd8c-9441b4b66de8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9957"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab2decf6-9a5b-5942-80b7-f48522e93b7f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051043}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184853}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Sine of a Large Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"} :step/id #uuid "daf93413-c40c-51c5-a21d-30d2ea7a31ea" :step/math-academy-id 37493 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184218 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184123 :question/id #uuid "07ae3d2b-9593-5927-ad3c-c5ad3682333f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9958"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a58fe278-8008-5dab-9b96-6ecda6202a2b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184489}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Cosine of a Large Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"} :step/id #uuid "6c7d9d02-5463-594d-84ef-eef4a1fa1587" :step/math-academy-id 37494 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184890}}] :activity/title "Extending the Trigonometric Ratios to Large Angles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184414}]}
